+    <section anchor="introduction" numbered="false" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-unnumbered-1">
+      <name slugifiedName="name-i-introduction">I. Introduction</name>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-unnumbered-1-1">Network hosts such as diskless workstations frequently do not know
 their protocol addresses when booted; they often know only their
 hardware interface addresses.  To communicate using higher-level
 protocols like IP, they must discover their protocol address from
 some external source.  Our problem is that there is no standard
 mechanism for doing so.</t>
-      <t indent="0" pn="section-i-2">Plummer's &quot;Address Resolution Protocol&quot; (ARP) [1] is designed to
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-unnumbered-1-2">Plummer's &quot;Address Resolution Protocol&quot; (ARP) <xref target="ref-1" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="1"/> is designed to
 solve a complementary problem, resolving a host's hardware address
 given its protocol address.  This RFC proposes a &quot;Reverse Address
 Resolution Protocol&quot; (RARP).  As with ARP, we assume a broadcast
 medium, such as Ethernet.</t>
     </section>
